dirk THANKS — work this project rests on.

The hard part of a multiplexer is terminal emulation, and it is a library.
What dirk owns is the window manager over the top; the parts underneath were
written by other people, and doing without any one of them would have meant a
year of someone else's debugging.

  vt100          Jesse Luehrs and contributors
                 The grid, the scrollback and the escape parsing. Everything
                 dirk knows about what a program drew, it learned from here.
                 <https://crates.io/crates/vt100>

  portable-pty   Wez Furlong and the wezterm contributors
                 Spawning a child on a pseudo-terminal, on both platforms,
                 correctly. The part everyone underestimates.
                 <https://crates.io/crates/portable-pty>

  ratatui        The ratatui contributors, and tui-rs before it
                 The chrome: layout, buffers and the widget model the sidebar
                 and the rail are built out of.
                 <https://ratatui.rs>

  crossterm      Timon Post and contributors
                 Raw mode, mouse reporting, and the same terminal behaviour on
                 Linux and macOS.
                 <https://crates.io/crates/crossterm>

  serde, toml, serde_json, chrono, unicode-width, libc
                 The unglamorous foundation. `unicode-width` in particular is
                 the reason the glyph table has one place to ask how wide a
                 character is, rather than several guesses.

Ideas rather than code:

  tmux           Nicholas Marriott
                 The prefix key, the split model, and thirty years of evidence
                 about which parts of a multiplexer people actually use. dirk
                 disagrees with tmux about the sidebar and about very little
                 else.

  GNU            The layout of this repository — COPYING, AUTHORS, NEWS,
                 INSTALL, THANKS, a ChangeLog generated rather than kept — is
                 the GNU coding standards', and it is a better answer than
                 anything this project would have invented.

  cairn          The roadmap and the issue tracker are the same files, in the
                 repository, reviewable in a pull request.
                 <https://github.com/oddurs/cairn>

  smali          The Gotham palette, and the sidebar and rail designs.
  namesync       The naming policy in src/name.rs is a port of it.
                 Both are the author's own earlier work, under the same terms;
                 see AUTHORS.

Bug reports, patches and the people who sent them are recorded in NEWS, release
by release. If you are missing from there and should not be, that is a bug;
please say so.


